WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:05.330
LEONARD LAW: Good
morning, everyone.

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:08.872
Hope you had a good
start to the show.

00:00:08.872 --> 00:00:09.830
My name is Leonard Law.

00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:12.121
I'm a product manager on the
Google Cloud Platform team

00:00:12.121 --> 00:00:14.990
and in my capacity,
I've had the privilege

00:00:14.990 --> 00:00:18.605
of working with many of our
largest enterprise customers.

00:00:18.605 --> 00:00:20.840
But a little bit about me.

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:25.280
Before joining Google, I've
worn a number of different hats.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:26.370
I was an entrepreneur.

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:30.345
I started a too early for
its time social media site.

00:00:30.345 --> 00:00:33.980
I was a consultant who
spent many years working

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:36.920
with a number of e-commerce
firms, an engineer building

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:39.720
many different applications
and technologies.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:42.110
And finally, for the decade
prior to joining Google,

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:44.360
I was a CIO on Wall Street.

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:46.006
And I tell you
this because I like

00:00:46.006 --> 00:00:48.380
to think that I have something
in common with many of you

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:49.580
in the room.

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:51.860
You know, I was in your
shoes, and for a long time,

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:53.990
I was a prospective
buyer of Cloud.

00:00:58.150 --> 00:01:03.690
Now I was interested in Cloud
as a CIO for quite a long time.

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:05.510
What I realized was
that I spent too much

00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:08.540
of my time and my budget
on the things that

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:10.960
were necessary to
keep the lights on.

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:13.790
And inversely, I
spent too little time

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:15.680
on things that really
would give my company

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:17.582
a competitive advantage.

00:01:17.582 --> 00:01:19.040
Cloud was this
thing that I thought

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:23.300
could unlock agility and
productivity and innovation,

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:25.880
but every time I tried to start
up a new Cloud initiative,

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:28.310
it felt like I got a roadblock
from somebody in Security

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:31.242
or Compliance or Risk.

00:01:31.242 --> 00:01:32.950
I want to take a quick
moment to tell you

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:35.170
a story that really
made the Cloud value

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:38.040
proposition real for me.

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:40.540
So as I mentioned, I was the
CIO of an investment management

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:41.039
firm.

00:01:41.039 --> 00:01:44.020
And as you might imagine, we
had to hold a lot of investor

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:47.954
records in our systems.

00:01:47.954 --> 00:01:49.620
Those investment
records included things

00:01:49.620 --> 00:01:55.051
like names, addresses, social
security numbers, tax IDs,

00:01:55.051 --> 00:01:57.300
stuff that you would classically
defined as personally

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:01.320
identifiable information.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:02.714
Of course, this is a nightmare.

00:02:02.714 --> 00:02:05.130
But we worked with our auditors
to come up with a strategy

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:06.660
to secure this data.

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:08.970
And what we did was
we encrypted all

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:12.480
of that data using Windows
Server 2003's Encrypting File

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:14.010
System.

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:15.840
We set up our records
management system

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:19.132
to be the only account that
had access to that certificate.

00:02:19.132 --> 00:02:21.590
Of course, you can imagine that
this story doesn't end well

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:24.742
and that certificate ended
up getting corrupted.

00:02:24.742 --> 00:02:26.450
And the lessons that
I learned after that

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:29.270
were pretty frightening.

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:32.950
So fortunately, we had a
backup of the private key

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:36.150
and that backup was in one of
my employees' personal Gmail

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:36.650
boxes.

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:42.650
It turned out the operations
manager, really nice guy,

00:02:42.650 --> 00:02:45.230
but he was really just
a glorified speed dial

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:47.674
to Microsoft Support.

00:02:47.674 --> 00:02:49.340
And it was at that
point that I realized

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:51.580
that Cloud was not
really nice to have,

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:53.100
it was a requirement for me.

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:54.890
Because at the end
of the day, there

00:02:54.890 --> 00:02:56.480
was no way that
my infrastructure

00:02:56.480 --> 00:02:59.390
folks would become sort
of a core competency

00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:01.102
for my business.

00:03:01.102 --> 00:03:02.560
And so Cloud was
something that was

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:05.170
much more than just
something to drive agility

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:07.390
and to create efficiency.

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:10.180
It was, in fact, something
that I needed to reduce risk

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:11.351
for my organization.

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:17.070
And so you have this sort
of vision of Cloud and Cloud

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:18.281
can be this amazing thing.

00:03:18.281 --> 00:03:18.780
Right?

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:20.910
It unlocks all
these opportunities.

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:23.820
I like to think about it
as Willy Wonka's chocolate

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:26.500
factory for an IT worker.

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:29.760
You can imagine this
world where infrastructure

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:32.620
can scale up from zero to
infinity and back on demand.

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:33.120
Right?

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:35.941
No more headaches
with procurement.

00:03:35.941 --> 00:03:37.440
Imagine a world
where you don't have

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:39.640
to worry about patch Tuesday.

00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:42.570
Software is upgraded
automatically,

00:03:42.570 --> 00:03:46.024
zero days are patched
on your behalf.

00:03:46.024 --> 00:03:47.940
Imagine a world where
your software developers

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:50.580
can focus on just writing
code, instead of trying

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:52.510
to run infrastructure.

00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:55.110
And finally, imagine a
world where I can actually

00:03:55.110 --> 00:03:59.170
make sense of all this data
that I've been collecting.

00:03:59.170 --> 00:04:01.400
In short, Cloud for
me, was something

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:04.640
that could truly transform
information technology

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:08.570
into business technology.

00:04:08.570 --> 00:04:11.130
But as you all know, it's
not such a simple story.

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:13.479
In fact, moving to
Cloud today can be hard.

00:04:13.479 --> 00:04:15.770
And so I'm going to spend
the rest of this talk talking

00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:18.440
about sort of three major
areas that companies

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:21.839
tend to think about when
initiating their move to Cloud.

00:04:21.839 --> 00:04:23.380
The first is security
and compliance,

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:26.970
and when I speak here,
mostly about data protection.

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:29.280
The next is on keeping
operational control

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:32.630
over a vast set of
Cloud resources.

00:04:32.630 --> 00:04:34.950
And the last is how do
you make sure Cloud can

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:36.330
work with my existing assets?

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:41.910
So over the last few
years, I'm sure you've seen

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:44.100
a headline like one of these.

00:04:44.100 --> 00:04:47.490
And nothing catches the
attention of an executive

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:51.160
or a board member like
an explosive headline.

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:55.030
Back in 2011, Experian conducted
a survey called the Reputation

00:04:55.030 --> 00:04:57.050
Impact of a Data Breach.

00:04:57.050 --> 00:04:59.650
And what they found was
that the average brand

00:04:59.650 --> 00:05:04.570
lost $184 to $332
million of brand value

00:05:04.570 --> 00:05:08.310
when incurring a major breach.

00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:10.750
So the first thing that
companies typically think about

00:05:10.750 --> 00:05:13.150
is data protection, or
as I like to call it,

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:15.010
how to keep the boss
out of jail and how

00:05:15.010 --> 00:05:17.205
to avoid making the
wrong headlines.

00:05:20.600 --> 00:05:22.190
So at Google, the
first thing we think

00:05:22.190 --> 00:05:23.773
about when it comes
to protecting data

00:05:23.773 --> 00:05:25.550
is encryption at rest.

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:28.400
And we believe this is an
imperative capability that

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:30.220
is required across
the entire platform.

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:34.880
And so at Google, all data is
encrypted at rest by default,

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:37.860
automatically.

00:05:37.860 --> 00:05:40.310
We also recognize,
though, that customers

00:05:40.310 --> 00:05:43.790
desire amount of control over
how their data is encrypted.

00:05:43.790 --> 00:05:46.430
And so we were fortunate to
work with a number of customers,

00:05:46.430 --> 00:05:49.190
including those from highly
regulated industries,

00:05:49.190 --> 00:05:51.830
to understand sort of the
trade offs between fully

00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:55.960
automated management and
fine grained control.

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:57.710
And so what we've done
is we've introduced

00:05:57.710 --> 00:05:59.550
a couple of new features.

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:05.090
The first is customer managed
keys using our Cloud KMS.

00:06:05.090 --> 00:06:08.150
And that's a model that allows
you to store and maintain

00:06:08.150 --> 00:06:11.870
keys in Google Cloud, but
also to still control things

00:06:11.870 --> 00:06:17.540
like rotation, creation,
revocation, and so on.

00:06:17.540 --> 00:06:20.950
And finally, we created customer
supplied encryption keys which

00:06:20.950 --> 00:06:24.610
is a novel feature
in a cloud platform

00:06:24.610 --> 00:06:27.130
which actually keeps encryption
keys only if I'm really

00:06:27.130 --> 00:06:28.240
in the Cloud.

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:30.156
What this means
is that customers

00:06:30.156 --> 00:06:31.780
can provide a key to
Google and encrypt

00:06:31.780 --> 00:06:33.790
the data on their
behalf using that key,

00:06:33.790 --> 00:06:36.250
but Google will never
persist that key on the cloud

00:06:36.250 --> 00:06:38.050
so that any time that
that data is at rest,

00:06:38.050 --> 00:06:41.440
Google has no access to
decrypting that data.

00:06:41.440 --> 00:06:43.320
I'm very pleased to
announce, or sorry,

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:46.860
to introduce Patrick
Lecuyer, who was formerly

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:50.130
the head of IaaS and PaaS
Security at Morgan Stanley

00:06:50.130 --> 00:06:53.100
and now the head
of cybersecurity

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:55.500
at Lightspeed HQ, who was
one of the customers who

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:58.435
helped us to come up with
some of these models.

00:06:58.435 --> 00:07:00.060
So Patrick's going
to talk a little bit

00:07:00.060 --> 00:07:05.770
about where he thinks these
use cases are appropriate.

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:08.010
PATRICK LECUYER:
Thank you, Leonard.

00:07:08.010 --> 00:07:11.810
So, yes, so I used to
work at Morgan Stanley,

00:07:11.810 --> 00:07:14.280
now at Lightspeed
HQ, so still in close

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:15.780
to the financial industry.

00:07:15.780 --> 00:07:17.600
At Morgan Stanley,
when we started

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:20.642
looking at moving
workloads to the cloud,

00:07:20.642 --> 00:07:22.100
one of the big
concerns that we had

00:07:22.100 --> 00:07:25.190
was around encryption
of confidential data.

00:07:25.190 --> 00:07:26.960
And the first
approach that we took

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:30.710
was to actually try to figure
out and choose the encryption

00:07:30.710 --> 00:07:32.390
controls we would
put in place in terms

00:07:32.390 --> 00:07:34.100
of data classification.

00:07:34.100 --> 00:07:37.170
So standard data classification
is either public, confidential,

00:07:37.170 --> 00:07:38.630
or restricted.

00:07:38.630 --> 00:07:42.590
And we very quickly figured
out that those classic data

00:07:42.590 --> 00:07:45.140
classification are
not all that relevant

00:07:45.140 --> 00:07:47.120
when you're talking about
encryption controls.

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:48.994
Because the reality is
that most of your data

00:07:48.994 --> 00:07:50.480
is confidential anyway.

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:52.742
You don't have that
much public data.

00:07:52.742 --> 00:07:54.950
And in order to figure out
what's the best encryption

00:07:54.950 --> 00:07:59.680
control to put in place, we
had to look more closely at how

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:01.520
we wanted to protect the data.

00:08:01.520 --> 00:08:04.790
So we figured that within that
large category of data that's

00:08:04.790 --> 00:08:06.770
confidential data,
we actually had

00:08:06.770 --> 00:08:09.920
different concerns around
different classes of data

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:11.100
within that.

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:14.060
And this is where the model
that Google is offering

00:08:14.060 --> 00:08:16.820
is actually extremely
interesting.

00:08:16.820 --> 00:08:19.190
And when we're
looking at that data,

00:08:19.190 --> 00:08:22.370
and if we move from the
fully automated mode

00:08:22.370 --> 00:08:25.310
all the way to the
finer grain, the default

00:08:25.310 --> 00:08:27.020
encryption that
Google offers actually

00:08:27.020 --> 00:08:29.960
allowed us to have basic
encryption interests.

00:08:29.960 --> 00:08:33.650
We call this the way that we
called it, and still call it,

00:08:33.650 --> 00:08:36.919
in the e-commerce industry,
that's compliance encryption.

00:08:36.919 --> 00:08:40.626
So your regulator asks you to
add something that's encrypted,

00:08:40.626 --> 00:08:41.750
you can say it's encrypted.

00:08:41.750 --> 00:08:46.490
And when you look at the white
papers that Google publish,

00:08:46.490 --> 00:08:48.830
it's actually very
well encrypted.

00:08:48.830 --> 00:08:51.152
You have a strong
key management.

00:08:51.152 --> 00:08:53.360
The thing it doesn't give
you is a lot of flexibility

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:55.276
or a lot of understanding
of what's happening.

00:08:55.276 --> 00:08:57.535
Encrypting is one
thing, but figuring out

00:08:57.535 --> 00:08:59.310
how the data is used
and figuring out

00:08:59.310 --> 00:09:01.490
how the keys are
released is actually

00:09:01.490 --> 00:09:04.220
much more important than
encrypting itself, especially

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:08.090
as you want to protect against
most sophisticated threats.

00:09:08.090 --> 00:09:10.850
So fine grained encryption,
OK for compliance,

00:09:10.850 --> 00:09:12.920
it protects against
the basic threats.

00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:14.510
It protects against
physical threats

00:09:14.510 --> 00:09:16.220
on Google's
infrastructure, it protects

00:09:16.220 --> 00:09:20.390
against disk based threats,
but as you move to data

00:09:20.390 --> 00:09:23.120
where you want to protect
against threats that would come

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:26.590
from somewhere else, you want
to move and have more visibility

00:09:26.590 --> 00:09:27.860
over that encryption.

00:09:27.860 --> 00:09:29.810
And this is where the
second model with Cloud

00:09:29.810 --> 00:09:32.420
came as a customer managed
encryption keys actually brings

00:09:32.420 --> 00:09:34.370
you a lot more visibility.

00:09:34.370 --> 00:09:36.650
Because right now,
at that point,

00:09:36.650 --> 00:09:38.560
Google doesn't control
the keys anymore.

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:40.280
They hold the key for
you, they give you

00:09:40.280 --> 00:09:45.860
a way to to segregate
duties to create auditing

00:09:45.860 --> 00:09:48.960
and to really understand
how your data is used.

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:51.710
So that's interesting because
one of the major threat that we

00:09:51.710 --> 00:09:53.744
figured out we wanted to
use encryption against

00:09:53.744 --> 00:09:55.160
was not all that
much threats that

00:09:55.160 --> 00:09:57.930
were coming from the
outside, but threats which

00:09:57.930 --> 00:09:59.660
were coming from the inside.

00:09:59.660 --> 00:10:04.560
We were more worried about our
own operation people making

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:07.470
the mistake and by
mistake, leaking data,

00:10:07.470 --> 00:10:10.070
that we were about a
sophisticated intruder trying

00:10:10.070 --> 00:10:11.820
to decrypt our data.

00:10:11.820 --> 00:10:15.560
And that's a concern that's
really particular to Cloud

00:10:15.560 --> 00:10:17.850
because we gain a lot
of agility in the cloud,

00:10:17.850 --> 00:10:20.840
but as we gained
that agility, we also

00:10:20.840 --> 00:10:24.740
end up losing some control
and we end up increasing risk

00:10:24.740 --> 00:10:26.360
that a mistake will happen.

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:29.540
And we found that by
using encryption and using

00:10:29.540 --> 00:10:32.720
some of the controls
around Cloud KMS,

00:10:32.720 --> 00:10:35.510
about how keys are released
and how we can segregate duties

00:10:35.510 --> 00:10:38.210
about people who can
encrypt and decrypt data,

00:10:38.210 --> 00:10:42.470
there is a way to prevent some
of the accidental data leak

00:10:42.470 --> 00:10:44.570
issues that might
come from the cloud.

00:10:44.570 --> 00:10:46.520
So that's very interesting.

00:10:46.520 --> 00:10:49.610
For most confidential data,
when you want to worry

00:10:49.610 --> 00:10:51.170
and make sure that your data--

00:10:51.170 --> 00:10:53.270
you will gain your
key visibility

00:10:53.270 --> 00:10:57.050
and you will be able
to keep disaggregation

00:10:57.050 --> 00:10:59.780
of duties that's extremely
hard to implement in Cloud.

00:10:59.780 --> 00:11:01.970
It's a very good model.

00:11:01.970 --> 00:11:06.260
And then we have the data that
leads to the type of headlines

00:11:06.260 --> 00:11:07.970
that was in previous slides.

00:11:07.970 --> 00:11:10.070
So there are datas,
and every industry

00:11:10.070 --> 00:11:12.660
has that data, that's
not only confidential,

00:11:12.660 --> 00:11:15.290
but has some regulatory
requirements around it.

00:11:15.290 --> 00:11:16.700
It might be your client data.

00:11:16.700 --> 00:11:18.700
It might be payment
card industry data.

00:11:18.700 --> 00:11:20.330
It may be health data.

00:11:20.330 --> 00:11:23.750
It's the kind of data
that if it ever gets out,

00:11:23.750 --> 00:11:26.030
you will need to answer
some very hard questions

00:11:26.030 --> 00:11:27.590
from your regulators.

00:11:27.590 --> 00:11:31.250
And this is where moving,
again, one way up the stack

00:11:31.250 --> 00:11:34.700
and moving from a place
where Google holds your key

00:11:34.700 --> 00:11:37.250
and gives you very
good auditing over them

00:11:37.250 --> 00:11:41.420
to a place where you actually
can manage your own key

00:11:41.420 --> 00:11:44.930
and provide the keys and keep
full control over the lifecycle

00:11:44.930 --> 00:11:50.300
of that keys, and providing
it when you make an action,

00:11:50.300 --> 00:11:53.660
and be able to scope that
key release to one very

00:11:53.660 --> 00:11:56.660
specific action that one
authorized user has taken,

00:11:56.660 --> 00:11:59.780
allowed us to answer
those questions,

00:11:59.780 --> 00:12:03.470
when or if they came,
way more precisely.

00:12:03.470 --> 00:12:06.020
Because now we add
full visibility

00:12:06.020 --> 00:12:09.270
over how the data was
being used because we were

00:12:09.270 --> 00:12:11.140
the one releasing the keys.

00:12:11.140 --> 00:12:14.040
So in a context where
you have regulators

00:12:14.040 --> 00:12:16.430
and you want to be able to
answer very quickly, OK, there

00:12:16.430 --> 00:12:18.930
was a data breach, could it have
come from the cloud provider?

00:12:18.930 --> 00:12:20.346
Could it have come
from a mistake?

00:12:20.346 --> 00:12:23.160
Could it have come from
an external attacker?

00:12:23.160 --> 00:12:26.160
Being able to control precisely
how that key is released

00:12:26.160 --> 00:12:29.340
is actually a very,
very strong control.

00:12:29.340 --> 00:12:32.670
But again, there's cost
to managing your own keys

00:12:32.670 --> 00:12:34.800
so finding that
right balance really

00:12:34.800 --> 00:12:36.840
depends on those
requirements and how

00:12:36.840 --> 00:12:40.330
you want to keep the visibility
to that control of your keys.

00:12:40.330 --> 00:12:44.430
So by looking at it
from a threat management

00:12:44.430 --> 00:12:46.830
point of view, rather than
from a data classification

00:12:46.830 --> 00:12:49.800
point of view, I think
it allows to have

00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:53.730
a much clearer vision on
how the keys should be used,

00:12:53.730 --> 00:12:54.960
should be managed.

00:12:54.960 --> 00:12:58.980
And this really allows
to align very efficiently

00:12:58.980 --> 00:13:03.330
with that model that
Google presents.

00:13:03.330 --> 00:13:04.880
So, yeah.

00:13:04.880 --> 00:13:05.380
Thank you.

00:13:05.380 --> 00:13:06.620
LEONARD LAW: Great.

00:13:06.620 --> 00:13:08.710
Thank you, Patrick.

00:13:08.710 --> 00:13:10.900
So as Patrick
described, you know

00:13:10.900 --> 00:13:15.310
encryption is a multi-faceted
decision that needs to be made.

00:13:15.310 --> 00:13:18.820
I encourage you all to
check out Session IO214,

00:13:18.820 --> 00:13:21.079
Managing Encryption
of Data on the Cloud.

00:13:21.079 --> 00:13:22.120
It's happening on Friday.

00:13:25.060 --> 00:13:27.020
OK.

00:13:27.020 --> 00:13:30.830
So let's say we've sorted
out our encryption strategy.

00:13:30.830 --> 00:13:33.710
It still may be the case that
we want to do a little bit more.

00:13:33.710 --> 00:13:36.074
Oftentimes, we may be
managing sensitive data.

00:13:36.074 --> 00:13:38.240
Maybe we don't know what
data is moving to the cloud

00:13:38.240 --> 00:13:41.450
and we need to figure out
how sensitive that data is.

00:13:41.450 --> 00:13:44.550
Maybe we are hosting
sensitive data in the cloud

00:13:44.550 --> 00:13:48.080
and we want to be careful that
that data doesn't leak out.

00:13:48.080 --> 00:13:50.930
One example might be you
have an e-commerce site

00:13:50.930 --> 00:13:53.610
and maybe, you've built
the customer service app,

00:13:53.610 --> 00:13:56.109
you want to make sure that that
customer service app doesn't

00:13:56.109 --> 00:14:00.350
accidentally allow a customer
service agent to leak PI

00:14:00.350 --> 00:14:02.957
information over online chat.

00:14:02.957 --> 00:14:05.290
For this, we have a number
of partners that can help us.

00:14:05.290 --> 00:14:06.790
And some very
exciting announcements

00:14:06.790 --> 00:14:08.789
that Diane mentioned will
be announced tomorrow,

00:14:08.789 --> 00:14:10.630
so I encourage you,
again, to come out

00:14:10.630 --> 00:14:16.258
to session IO218 which will be
happening tomorrow afternoon.

00:14:19.400 --> 00:14:22.910
And I would be remiss to talk
about in any kind of migration

00:14:22.910 --> 00:14:26.582
story without giving
a nod to our partners.

00:14:26.582 --> 00:14:28.040
So in the space of
data protection,

00:14:28.040 --> 00:14:31.760
we have a number a selection
of partners that we work with.

00:14:31.760 --> 00:14:34.590
For example, IONIC Security,
which is a Google Ventures

00:14:34.590 --> 00:14:36.647
backed company,
provides high assurance

00:14:36.647 --> 00:14:39.230
and high throughput encryption
key and entitlements management

00:14:39.230 --> 00:14:41.420
solutions for
global organizations

00:14:41.420 --> 00:14:46.790
like Goldman Sachs, Aetna, ADP,
and the Department of Defense.

00:14:46.790 --> 00:14:50.390
One of their products, IONIC
Protect for Cloud Storage,

00:14:50.390 --> 00:14:53.690
enables developers to simply
and seamlessly use their own one

00:14:53.690 --> 00:14:56.690
time use encryption keys
with the full capabilities

00:14:56.690 --> 00:14:59.390
of the IONIC platform,
but integrate it natively

00:14:59.390 --> 00:15:02.020
with Google Cloud Storage.

00:15:02.020 --> 00:15:05.410
Another partner, KeyNexus,
provides centralized platform

00:15:05.410 --> 00:15:07.390
agnostic key management
solution that

00:15:07.390 --> 00:15:09.370
can be deployed on a
variety of architectures,

00:15:09.370 --> 00:15:11.380
both in the cloud
and on premise,

00:15:11.380 --> 00:15:13.540
and it helps to enable
a variety of bring

00:15:13.540 --> 00:15:15.220
your own key scenarios.

00:15:15.220 --> 00:15:18.070
KeyNexus integrates
directly with GCP,

00:15:18.070 --> 00:15:21.280
including our G Cloud client
interface for Google Compute

00:15:21.280 --> 00:15:25.510
Engine, as well as the
gsutil SDK for Cloud Storage.

00:15:25.510 --> 00:15:28.527
In both cases, this allows
customers to leverage

00:15:28.527 --> 00:15:30.610
the customer supplied
encryption key functionality

00:15:30.610 --> 00:15:33.310
that I talked about,
but using keys

00:15:33.310 --> 00:15:35.890
that are stored in
KeyNexus and allowing

00:15:35.890 --> 00:15:38.095
for the rotation and
federated management

00:15:38.095 --> 00:15:41.280
that pattern provides.

00:15:41.280 --> 00:15:44.140
From here, I'd like to
introduce Sol Cates.

00:15:44.140 --> 00:15:47.417
Sol is the Vice President of
Technical Strategy at Thales

00:15:47.417 --> 00:15:49.750
and he's going to spend a few
minutes talking about what

00:15:49.750 --> 00:15:52.090
their company brings to bear.

00:15:52.090 --> 00:15:54.010
SOL CATES: Thank you.

00:15:54.010 --> 00:15:55.450
Hello?

00:15:55.450 --> 00:15:56.900
Hello?

00:15:56.900 --> 00:15:57.680
Hello, computer.

00:15:57.680 --> 00:15:58.640
There we go.

00:15:58.640 --> 00:15:59.490
Excellent.

00:15:59.490 --> 00:16:01.637
So as Leonard said,
I'm Sol Cates.

00:16:01.637 --> 00:16:03.470
I'm the VP of Technical
Strategy for Thales.

00:16:03.470 --> 00:16:05.630
Prior to the acquisition,
I was with a company

00:16:05.630 --> 00:16:09.090
called Vormetric which some
of you might be familiar with.

00:16:09.090 --> 00:16:11.150
And I want to talk
about two sort of things

00:16:11.150 --> 00:16:15.950
that are interesting to us in
conjunction with Google Cloud.

00:16:15.950 --> 00:16:18.950
So as most of you
know, going to Cloud

00:16:18.950 --> 00:16:20.210
is a shared responsibility.

00:16:20.210 --> 00:16:24.350
I was a CSO for many years
so I also agree with Leonard.

00:16:24.350 --> 00:16:26.870
One of the challenges as
you go to new technologies,

00:16:26.870 --> 00:16:29.210
like Cloud in particular,
it's figuring out

00:16:29.210 --> 00:16:32.270
where the line in the
sand from innovation

00:16:32.270 --> 00:16:34.497
versus control perspective.

00:16:34.497 --> 00:16:36.830
How do we make sure that we're
leveraging the technology

00:16:36.830 --> 00:16:38.510
effectively, getting
the value we need out

00:16:38.510 --> 00:16:40.850
of it as quickly as possible,
and making sure we're not

00:16:40.850 --> 00:16:44.310
running into a burning fire?

00:16:44.310 --> 00:16:46.980
So if you guys haven't seen
the Cloud Security Alliance,

00:16:46.980 --> 00:16:50.490
I highly recommend you
research them a little bit.

00:16:50.490 --> 00:16:53.730
They have a lot of great models
about how to do Cloud securely,

00:16:53.730 --> 00:16:55.230
particularly when
it comes to things

00:16:55.230 --> 00:16:56.710
like shared responsibilities.

00:16:56.710 --> 00:17:00.030
What is your responsibility as
the buyer of this technology?

00:17:00.030 --> 00:17:03.090
What are you supposed to operate
from a control perspective?

00:17:03.090 --> 00:17:05.460
All the way from SaaS platforms
down to infrastructure

00:17:05.460 --> 00:17:07.260
as a service, so it's a
very good resource if you

00:17:07.260 --> 00:17:08.301
haven't looked at it yet.

00:17:08.301 --> 00:17:09.810
And the reason I
bring it up is I

00:17:09.810 --> 00:17:12.839
see most Cloud
buyers traditionally

00:17:12.839 --> 00:17:15.670
either came from the IT
side and not security.

00:17:15.670 --> 00:17:19.230
So when they're looking for how
do I actually successfully move

00:17:19.230 --> 00:17:20.802
to Cloud securely,
having resources

00:17:20.802 --> 00:17:23.010
like the Cloud Security
Alliance is a very, very good

00:17:23.010 --> 00:17:23.730
first step.

00:17:23.730 --> 00:17:27.125
And they got tons of great
tools for you to use.

00:17:27.125 --> 00:17:29.000
So I did actually want
to talk a little about

00:17:29.000 --> 00:17:31.770
a couple announcements
that we have with Google.

00:17:31.770 --> 00:17:34.830
We have just launched
our Vormetric Transparent

00:17:34.830 --> 00:17:39.584
Encryption technology into
the Test Drive capability.

00:17:39.584 --> 00:17:42.000
So up on Orbitera, so you can
go check it out and actually

00:17:42.000 --> 00:17:44.834
play with a transparent
encryption technology

00:17:44.834 --> 00:17:46.500
that we've been using
for about 15 years

00:17:46.500 --> 00:17:47.958
to solve a fundamental
problem I'll

00:17:47.958 --> 00:17:49.960
talk about here in a minute.

00:17:49.960 --> 00:17:52.200
And then we also supported
the bring your own key

00:17:52.200 --> 00:17:53.910
for the ephemeral
keys that are going

00:17:53.910 --> 00:17:55.500
into the customer supplied keys.

00:17:55.500 --> 00:17:58.470
So you can actually
back the key material

00:17:58.470 --> 00:18:02.550
that you're using to encrypt
data inside your instances

00:18:02.550 --> 00:18:06.940
and drives up within Google
with HSM backed cryptography.

00:18:06.940 --> 00:18:10.890
The keys never actually leave
that HSM without being wrapped

00:18:10.890 --> 00:18:13.920
and then they're imported
into the cloud provider,

00:18:13.920 --> 00:18:15.780
in this case Google,
in ephemeral ways

00:18:15.780 --> 00:18:20.360
so the data and the
keys are separated.

00:18:20.360 --> 00:18:22.850
So Thales as a
company is fairly big.

00:18:22.850 --> 00:18:24.590
We do a lot of big things.

00:18:24.590 --> 00:18:26.060
We throw stuff into space.

00:18:26.060 --> 00:18:28.640
We build aircraft carriers.

00:18:28.640 --> 00:18:31.634
But we also
specialize in security

00:18:31.634 --> 00:18:33.175
and we have a fairly
large portfolio.

00:18:33.175 --> 00:18:34.841
I'm going to focus
on a couple of things

00:18:34.841 --> 00:18:36.770
today that might be of
interest, particularly

00:18:36.770 --> 00:18:39.220
on that gradient scale of
security we were just talking

00:18:39.220 --> 00:18:42.140
about a couple minutes ago,
everything from compliance

00:18:42.140 --> 00:18:45.020
encryption all the way up to
that very, very tactical, very

00:18:45.020 --> 00:18:49.170
sensitive information I had
to be very concerned with.

00:18:49.170 --> 00:18:51.170
And one of the technologies
I wanted to focus on

00:18:51.170 --> 00:18:52.730
is Vormetric
Transparent Encryption.

00:18:52.730 --> 00:18:54.688
It's something that's
been around for about 15,

00:18:54.688 --> 00:18:56.209
16 years now.

00:18:56.209 --> 00:18:58.250
And just because it's old,
doesn't mean it's bad.

00:18:58.250 --> 00:19:01.250
We sort of got the architecture
the right way the first time.

00:19:01.250 --> 00:19:04.040
We had designed a
technology to help combat

00:19:04.040 --> 00:19:05.800
a very serious
threat that we saw

00:19:05.800 --> 00:19:08.570
within the infrastructure
of most IT departments

00:19:08.570 --> 00:19:11.910
and that is root, the God
of the operating system,

00:19:11.910 --> 00:19:14.220
the DBA of the database.

00:19:14.220 --> 00:19:14.720
Right?

00:19:14.720 --> 00:19:17.720
Those privileged users had
unfettered access to the data

00:19:17.720 --> 00:19:21.650
and we'll talk a little
bit about why that is.

00:19:21.650 --> 00:19:24.360
So the transparent
encryption technology

00:19:24.360 --> 00:19:25.820
has been around
for quite a while.

00:19:25.820 --> 00:19:28.476
It focuses on just about
any kind of data type,

00:19:28.476 --> 00:19:29.600
any kind of infrastructure.

00:19:29.600 --> 00:19:30.980
It can run in Cloud.

00:19:30.980 --> 00:19:33.530
It can run in hybrid mode,
the unphysical systems,

00:19:33.530 --> 00:19:35.900
legacy platforms,
next generation,

00:19:35.900 --> 00:19:37.960
container backed applications.

00:19:37.960 --> 00:19:38.840
It's fairly flexible.

00:19:38.840 --> 00:19:41.300
We designed to be
so many years ago.

00:19:41.300 --> 00:19:42.710
And as new
innovations happen, we

00:19:42.710 --> 00:19:45.890
make sure that we
embrace and support them.

00:19:45.890 --> 00:19:48.140
And really the notion
is data should always

00:19:48.140 --> 00:19:49.560
be in a protected state.

00:19:49.560 --> 00:19:51.040
I really like the
fact that Google

00:19:51.040 --> 00:19:54.470
is taking a stance as well
as, at the minimum, encrypted

00:19:54.470 --> 00:19:54.980
at rest.

00:19:54.980 --> 00:19:55.480
Right?

00:19:55.480 --> 00:19:58.290
Even if it's a transparent key
that's governed to your tenant,

00:19:58.290 --> 00:19:59.480
that's better than zero.

00:19:59.480 --> 00:19:59.980
Right?

00:19:59.980 --> 00:20:01.406
So it's slightly
better than zero.

00:20:01.406 --> 00:20:02.780
Then you have that
gradient scale

00:20:02.780 --> 00:20:05.155
to get all the way up to that
very sensitive information,

00:20:05.155 --> 00:20:06.890
because I've been
doing this for 15

00:20:06.890 --> 00:20:11.060
years in different capacities
in the encryption space,

00:20:11.060 --> 00:20:13.440
no one does encryption
because they want to.

00:20:13.440 --> 00:20:15.050
It's something
compels them to do so.

00:20:15.050 --> 00:20:17.555
It's either a risk reduction,
it's a compliance requirement,

00:20:17.555 --> 00:20:21.545
it's a customer screaming at
them, some reason to change.

00:20:21.545 --> 00:20:24.830
And encryption is actually
not the hardest part to do.

00:20:24.830 --> 00:20:26.240
That's the easiest thing to do.

00:20:26.240 --> 00:20:27.790
It's all the
complexity around it--

00:20:27.790 --> 00:20:29.810
key management,
separation of duties,

00:20:29.810 --> 00:20:32.229
who can see this data,
under what conditions,

00:20:32.229 --> 00:20:33.020
so on and so forth.

00:20:33.020 --> 00:20:36.320
So we built a technology to
combat the privileged user,

00:20:36.320 --> 00:20:38.690
in particular, in
the Unix environment

00:20:38.690 --> 00:20:41.690
at the very beginning,
but then we actually

00:20:41.690 --> 00:20:43.130
enhanced it over
the last 15 years

00:20:43.130 --> 00:20:46.010
to be very transparent
and extendable.

00:20:46.010 --> 00:20:49.760
So what we do is we encrypt data
at rest inside your operating

00:20:49.760 --> 00:20:50.507
systems and VMs.

00:20:50.507 --> 00:20:51.965
And again, this is
just one product

00:20:51.965 --> 00:20:54.470
out of the multitude
of the portfolio

00:20:54.470 --> 00:20:56.720
because we can go all the
way up to tokenization.

00:20:56.720 --> 00:21:01.070
But a lot of our organizations
like to focus on need to know.

00:21:01.070 --> 00:21:03.770
If you guys ever worked in
the intelligence community

00:21:03.770 --> 00:21:05.870
or worked in DoD
or any place where

00:21:05.870 --> 00:21:07.370
you have these
compartmentalisation

00:21:07.370 --> 00:21:09.290
of different types of
data, you understand

00:21:09.290 --> 00:21:12.770
the concept of having the need
to know or the right to know.

00:21:12.770 --> 00:21:14.420
And what we did was
build a technology

00:21:14.420 --> 00:21:16.628
that actually encrypts data
at rest and then to leave

00:21:16.628 --> 00:21:17.670
that state of encryption.

00:21:17.670 --> 00:21:19.794
You have to be the right
user doing the right thing

00:21:19.794 --> 00:21:21.830
the right way with the
right authentication.

00:21:21.830 --> 00:21:24.220
It's a policy based
approach to saying

00:21:24.220 --> 00:21:25.700
who is the right
user of this data

00:21:25.700 --> 00:21:27.380
and when should they see it?

00:21:27.380 --> 00:21:30.825
So we just launched this in
Test Drive this last week.

00:21:30.825 --> 00:21:33.200
So you can now actually go
out and play with it, but what

00:21:33.200 --> 00:21:36.020
this does it actually allows
you, through policy, to say

00:21:36.020 --> 00:21:38.600
which users on this platform
should ever see this data.

00:21:38.600 --> 00:21:41.370
And a user, nine times out
of 10, is an application.

00:21:41.370 --> 00:21:41.870
All right?

00:21:41.870 --> 00:21:42.942
It's a container.

00:21:42.942 --> 00:21:44.150
Which data should I be using?

00:21:44.150 --> 00:21:46.516
Which service account should
access this information?

00:21:46.516 --> 00:21:48.140
And the reason it's
important is if you

00:21:48.140 --> 00:21:50.473
look at every single data
breach you probably read about

00:21:50.473 --> 00:21:51.974
in the last five,
six years, there's

00:21:51.974 --> 00:21:54.264
a common thing that happens
at the very end of the data

00:21:54.264 --> 00:21:54.920
breach--

00:21:54.920 --> 00:21:56.930
escalate privilege, steal data.

00:21:56.930 --> 00:21:59.270
Well, we took the privilege
away many years ago.

00:21:59.270 --> 00:22:00.710
If you employ
something like this,

00:22:00.710 --> 00:22:04.400
it allows you to get much
more granular as to what

00:22:04.400 --> 00:22:09.090
and who and why information
can be accessed in a clear way.

00:22:09.090 --> 00:22:09.590
Right?

00:22:09.590 --> 00:22:11.690
The default stance
is keep it protected.

00:22:11.690 --> 00:22:13.840
To leave this stance,
you've got to authenticate

00:22:13.840 --> 00:22:17.010
the appropriate way.

00:22:17.010 --> 00:22:19.380
And then with that, again,
another part of the portfolio

00:22:19.380 --> 00:22:20.754
is we do a lot of
key management.

00:22:20.754 --> 00:22:22.830
So we've just put
some investments

00:22:22.830 --> 00:22:25.830
in over the last six months
or so to actually support

00:22:25.830 --> 00:22:28.530
bringing your own key into
multiple cloud providers.

00:22:28.530 --> 00:22:29.790
We see a common problem here.

00:22:29.790 --> 00:22:31.630
This is sort of the
way that my team works.

00:22:31.630 --> 00:22:34.140
We start with the problem, we
work towards the technology.

00:22:34.140 --> 00:22:36.556
There is a common problem,
it's just cloud key management.

00:22:36.556 --> 00:22:38.040
As cloud providers,
such as Google,

00:22:38.040 --> 00:22:40.620
are turning on more and
more encryption capabilities

00:22:40.620 --> 00:22:44.130
within their frameworks,
the need for the enterprise

00:22:44.130 --> 00:22:48.540
to actually manage those keys
is growing, it's increasing.

00:22:48.540 --> 00:22:50.339
But the challenge is
every single provider

00:22:50.339 --> 00:22:51.630
is doing it slightly different.

00:22:51.630 --> 00:22:54.630
They have different concepts,
different architectures,

00:22:54.630 --> 00:22:56.520
different APIs you work with.

00:22:56.520 --> 00:22:58.560
So we actually invested
in creative technology

00:22:58.560 --> 00:23:01.500
what we called our key
management as a service that

00:23:01.500 --> 00:23:04.860
actually allows you to back
all these key managers as they

00:23:04.860 --> 00:23:07.590
evolve over the
next couple of years

00:23:07.590 --> 00:23:10.750
from one single pane of glass
with keys managed and stored

00:23:10.750 --> 00:23:11.760
on prem.

00:23:11.760 --> 00:23:15.090
So you have that flexibility
of [INAUDIBLE] in a cloud using

00:23:15.090 --> 00:23:18.180
those infrastructure,
that service, those paths,

00:23:18.180 --> 00:23:21.300
applications that you would
like to, and ground it

00:23:21.300 --> 00:23:23.310
in key material, and
governance is done

00:23:23.310 --> 00:23:25.757
on prem in traditional
HSMs and then,

00:23:25.757 --> 00:23:27.840
of course, our Data Security
Manager which is part

00:23:27.840 --> 00:23:30.720
of the Vormetric portfolio.

00:23:30.720 --> 00:23:32.670
And with that, thank you.

00:23:32.670 --> 00:23:36.020
And if you want to come chat,
we have a booth here, C3.

00:23:36.020 --> 00:23:37.859
Thank you.

00:23:37.859 --> 00:23:38.650
LEONARD LAW: Great.

00:23:38.650 --> 00:23:39.610
Thanks, Sol.

00:23:39.610 --> 00:23:41.530
[APPLAUSE]

00:23:45.850 --> 00:23:49.000
So let's assume that we've
made some decisions around data

00:23:49.000 --> 00:23:51.080
protection.

00:23:51.080 --> 00:23:54.020
The next thing that really
scares a lot of people

00:23:54.020 --> 00:23:57.330
is how do we manage
these resources at scale.

00:23:57.330 --> 00:23:58.612
Right?

00:23:58.612 --> 00:24:00.320
One of the nightmares
that I used to have

00:24:00.320 --> 00:24:04.820
is the 100 tab spreadsheet
that my auditors gave me

00:24:04.820 --> 00:24:06.800
for SOX compliance.

00:24:06.800 --> 00:24:08.480
And imagine applying
that spreadsheet

00:24:08.480 --> 00:24:11.294
to the sort of
limitless resources

00:24:11.294 --> 00:24:12.710
that are afforded
to you in Cloud.

00:24:12.710 --> 00:24:15.860
It's not a good place to be.

00:24:15.860 --> 00:24:18.950
And so clearly, being able
to manage resources at scale

00:24:18.950 --> 00:24:22.520
is imperative to controlling the
risk as you move into the cloud

00:24:22.520 --> 00:24:23.303
environment.

00:24:26.480 --> 00:24:29.030
And so I want to talk
about a couple of things.

00:24:29.030 --> 00:24:30.560
The first step that
you want to take

00:24:30.560 --> 00:24:32.600
when trying to control
resources in Google Cloud

00:24:32.600 --> 00:24:35.084
is to start with the
access management patterns

00:24:35.084 --> 00:24:37.000
that you're going to
apply for your resources.

00:24:37.000 --> 00:24:39.680
And so what you can
do is in Cloud IAM,

00:24:39.680 --> 00:24:42.250
you can set up fine grained
roles and permissions

00:24:42.250 --> 00:24:45.980
that define the patterns
that can access your cloud

00:24:45.980 --> 00:24:48.530
resources.

00:24:48.530 --> 00:24:50.960
Next, by grouping those
resources into our resource

00:24:50.960 --> 00:24:54.580
hierarchy, you can create
some organizational constructs

00:24:54.580 --> 00:24:56.660
and that logical
grouping allows you

00:24:56.660 --> 00:25:03.050
to then apply those actions and
policies in an inherited way.

00:25:03.050 --> 00:25:04.820
And finally, at
the top level, we

00:25:04.820 --> 00:25:06.950
have these organization
wide policies

00:25:06.950 --> 00:25:08.930
that can help you
to create limits

00:25:08.930 --> 00:25:11.840
that enforce certain constructs
within your Cloud platform

00:25:11.840 --> 00:25:12.800
deployment.

00:25:12.800 --> 00:25:14.930
For example, you might want
to create a policy that

00:25:14.930 --> 00:25:18.050
says under no circumstances
should a user be

00:25:18.050 --> 00:25:21.095
able to have serial port access
to a Compute Engine instance.

00:25:21.095 --> 00:25:23.470
And that's something you would
set at a high level policy

00:25:23.470 --> 00:25:25.640
level.

00:25:25.640 --> 00:25:28.240
We believe very much in sort
of a belt and suspenders

00:25:28.240 --> 00:25:30.310
model for security,
and so in addition

00:25:30.310 --> 00:25:31.960
to being able to
set the policy, we

00:25:31.960 --> 00:25:34.330
want to be able to verify the
activity that's happening.

00:25:34.330 --> 00:25:37.549
So GCP provides a very robust
audit logging capabilities

00:25:37.549 --> 00:25:40.090
which keeps track of both your
administrative and potentially

00:25:40.090 --> 00:25:45.530
data access events that are
happening on Google Cloud.

00:25:45.530 --> 00:25:48.360
Again, there's going to be a
much deeper dive on this topic,

00:25:48.360 --> 00:25:51.230
please check out
session IO211 for how

00:25:51.230 --> 00:25:53.917
to control your organization's
Cloud resources.

00:25:53.917 --> 00:25:55.250
It's going to be a good session.

00:25:59.120 --> 00:26:01.160
I want to go back to
partners again here.

00:26:01.160 --> 00:26:03.480
In this particular
space, I think partners

00:26:03.480 --> 00:26:05.390
are especially important.

00:26:05.390 --> 00:26:08.030
As customers are looking at
hybrid cloud and multi cloud

00:26:08.030 --> 00:26:09.620
worlds, it's very
likely that they

00:26:09.620 --> 00:26:12.650
have resources not just
in GCP, but also spread

00:26:12.650 --> 00:26:16.910
across multiple infrastructures.

00:26:16.910 --> 00:26:18.440
So take, for example, Qualys.

00:26:18.440 --> 00:26:20.570
Qualys provides their
virtual scanner appliance

00:26:20.570 --> 00:26:23.414
for GCP which extends the
same multilayered security

00:26:23.414 --> 00:26:25.580
that customers are used to
running in their own data

00:26:25.580 --> 00:26:27.770
centers to Google cloud.

00:26:27.770 --> 00:26:30.200
Users can now scan
GCP resources using

00:26:30.200 --> 00:26:32.690
both multi Cloud and
