mostly open source,

00:30:06.960 --> 00:30:08.210
but this doesn't really mean--

00:30:08.210 --> 00:30:11.280
MongoDB Server,
Elasticsearch Server

00:30:11.280 --> 00:30:14.490
being deployed to
the public cloud

00:30:14.490 --> 00:30:18.510
said they're not properly
configured, totally accessible

00:30:18.510 --> 00:30:20.970
from the entire internet.

00:30:20.970 --> 00:30:25.390
And then with some lack
of authentication or not

00:30:25.390 --> 00:30:28.770
too strong authentication, it
was hijacked by the attacker

00:30:28.770 --> 00:30:32.140
and then ransomwared,
et cetera, et cetera.

00:30:32.140 --> 00:30:35.310
And that kind of leads
me to the first issue

00:30:35.310 --> 00:30:37.410
with maintaining
workloads in the cloud,

00:30:37.410 --> 00:30:39.610
the operational visibility.

00:30:39.610 --> 00:30:42.180
What's my network exposure?

00:30:42.180 --> 00:30:43.740
What's my DMZ?

00:30:43.740 --> 00:30:47.470
And where are my
IT assets at all?

00:30:47.470 --> 00:30:51.030
And even if we identify a
specific critical element

00:30:51.030 --> 00:30:54.030
like this MongoDB server,
what's the effective security

00:30:54.030 --> 00:30:57.740
posture, network security
posture, of that element?

00:30:57.740 --> 00:30:59.970
With that, I would
like to show you

00:30:59.970 --> 00:31:03.240
the Dome9 system and
how we help protecting

00:31:03.240 --> 00:31:05.100
against these kinds of issues.

00:31:05.100 --> 00:31:06.220
Let's switch to the demo.

00:31:10.530 --> 00:31:11.390
OK.

00:31:11.390 --> 00:31:15.860
So what you're seeing now
