it's called Clarity which

00:31:20.240 --> 00:31:24.500
now connects natively
to the GCP APIs,

00:31:24.500 --> 00:31:27.800
process all the workloads,
configuration policies,

00:31:27.800 --> 00:31:31.700
and then builds these kind
of visual map of your assets

00:31:31.700 --> 00:31:34.230
and their exposure.

00:31:34.230 --> 00:31:36.740
So right now,
trying to understand

00:31:36.740 --> 00:31:40.490
what is my DMZ is a
symbol just looking

00:31:40.490 --> 00:31:44.840
at the swim lane of the DMZ or
clicking on this internet node

00:31:44.840 --> 00:31:48.100
and seeing all the
members, all my assets

00:31:48.100 --> 00:31:50.970
there are exposed directly
to the entire internet.

00:31:50.970 --> 00:31:54.050
So kind of understanding
that they have this Mongo

00:31:54.050 --> 00:31:59.340
member here with public IP
address and this port TCP

00:31:59.340 --> 00:32:04.850
27017, the MongoDB port,
talking with the entire internet

00:32:04.850 --> 00:32:07.850
is now a trivial thing.

00:32:07.850 --> 00:32:12.020
Now that system also allows
you to understand and analyze

00:32:12.020 --> 00:32:16.220
the firewall rules, the GCP
firewall rules, and the subnets

00:32:16.220 --> 00:32:19.610
in order to really understand
how different assets

00:32:19.610 --> 00:32:21.980
communicate between themselves.

00:32:21.980 --> 00:32:25.280
So really allowing you
to tighten these micro

00:32:25.280 --> 00:32:27.570
segmentation-- it's
a nice buzzword--

00:32:27.570 --> 00:32:29.480
so tighten this
up and really make

00:32:29.480 --> 00:32:32.810
sure you've got your
security interconnectivity

00:32:32.810 --> 00:32:37.290
between your intercomponents
kind of [INAUDIBLE].

00:32:37.290 --> 00:32:39.475
Let's move back to
the presentation.

00:32:42.940 --> 00:32:43.440
OK.

00:32:43.440 --> 00:32:45.450
The next topic I
would like to attach

00:32:45.450 --> 00:32:48.960
is compliance and governance.

00:32:48.960 --> 00:32:53.250
Although this is an old
topic, but transferring that

00:32:53.250 --> 00:32:56.470
into the cloud brings
new challenges.

00:32:56.470 --> 00:32:59.820
So how do I adhere to my
existing complex regulations--

00:32:59.820 --> 00:33:02.370
BCI, HIPAA, you name it?

00:33:02.370 --> 00:33:05.400
And on top of that,
also, how do I

00:33:05.400 --> 00:33:09.480
adhere to my internal
corporate ID policies?

00:33:09.480 --> 00:33:15.490
And how do I translate those
policies to my GCP environment?

00:33:15.490 --> 00:33:18.730
So I would like to introduce
you to the Dome9 Compliance

00:33:18.730 --> 00:33:21.940
Engine which is an automated
compliance and governance

00:33:21.940 --> 00:33:25.620
system that allows you
to test your GCP project,

00:33:25.620 --> 00:33:29.320
your GCP environments against
industry best practices,

00:33:29.320 --> 00:33:32.840
against complex
regulations, and in turn,

00:33:32.840 --> 00:33:35.620
your own custom
internal IT policies.

00:33:35.620 --> 00:33:37.235
Let's see that in action.

00:33:37.235 --> 00:33:38.360
Switch to the demo, please.

00:33:42.810 --> 00:33:43.760
OK.

00:33:43.760 --> 00:33:46.250
So here we are seeing the
compliance and governance

00:33:46.250 --> 00:33:50.360
viewing the Dome9 ARC
platform and we're

00:33:50.360 --> 00:33:55.580
going to pick from a
number of policies,

00:33:55.580 --> 00:33:57.410
we call them bundles--

00:33:57.410 --> 00:33:59.570
just container for rules.

00:33:59.570 --> 00:34:03.920
And I'll take the best
practices for the GCP bundle

00:34:03.920 --> 00:34:06.200
and I would like to
run an assessment

00:34:06.200 --> 00:34:10.969
against one of my GCP projects.

00:34:10.969 --> 00:34:14.790
So now Dome9, in the
background, performing

00:34:14.790 --> 00:34:18.449
hundreds of tests against
my assets and my policies.

00:34:18.449 --> 00:34:23.489
And almost in less than 10
seconds, gave me that report.

00:34:23.489 --> 00:34:25.980
Let me drill down
into the failed tests

00:34:25.980 --> 00:34:28.739
and into the high severity ones.

00:34:28.739 --> 00:34:32.460
And I can see that I
have these Mongo member

00:34:32.460 --> 00:34:38.790
backup in the Western Union
kind of automatically detected

00:34:38.790 --> 00:34:42.120
by the system.

00:34:42.120 --> 00:34:45.989
As I promise, it's not only
testing against Dome9 best

00:34:45.989 --> 00:34:48.000
practices or industry
best practices,

00:34:48.000 --> 00:34:52.469
it can also allow you
to model your own best

00:34:52.469 --> 00:34:56.800
practices, your own
internal IT policies

00:34:56.800 --> 00:34:58.830
and model them in the cloud.

00:34:58.830 --> 00:35:03.900
So as just an example, I created
a little policy with only two

00:35:03.900 --> 00:35:07.980
tests that allows my instances--
let me try to increase that--

00:35:07.980 --> 00:35:11.250
allows my instances to only
operate on specific user

00:35:11.250 --> 00:35:15.570
regions or that no firewall
rules should allow us

00:35:15.570 --> 00:35:17.170
to say to the entire internet.

00:35:17.170 --> 00:35:19.830
Now these rules,
those policies, are

00:35:19.830 --> 00:35:23.370
created in what we call GSL,
the Governance Specification

00:35:23.370 --> 00:35:26.490
Language, which allows
you to create something

00:35:26.490 --> 00:35:30.530
like English SQL
kind of statements

00:35:30.530 --> 00:35:34.590
and that allow you to create
your own specific custom

00:35:34.590 --> 00:35:37.450
governance, too.

